      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Find the general solution to the differential equation $y′=\\left(12 x - 36\\right)\\sqrt[3]{y^2}.$ Work on intervals where $y\\ne 0$; cube roots are real.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y′ & =\\left(12 x - 36\\right)\\sqrt[3]{y^2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{y^2}}\\,\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} & =4 x - 12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{y^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}y & =\\int 4 x - 12\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{y} & =2 \\left(x - 3\\right)^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution in implicit form is\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{y}=2 \\left(x - 3\\right)^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nWe're required to write the solution in the form $y=y(x).$ Solving for $y$ gives\n\n$$\ny=\\left(C + 2 \\left(x - 3\\right)^{2}\\right)^{3}.\n$$\n\nHere $C$ is an arbitrary real constant.",
